window.COURSE = {"title": "Quantum Machine Learning", "chapter": {"id": "classical-information", "title": "Classical Systems and Information", "number": 1, "part": "Part I · Quantum computing", "body_file": "chapters/classical-information/overview.html", "body": "<p class=\"lead\">Before asking what makes a quantum computer different, let us build a language for systems we already understand. A switch, a die and a sealed result envelope will take us from state labels to probability columns, operation matrices and complete calculations.</p>\n<p>The chapter follows the lecture's systems-first approach: ask what information we need, choose a representation, work through a small example, and explain what the answer means. The theory, derivations and worked reasoning form the main reading path. Experiments and programming let us check our predictions.</p>\n<aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>Before we begin</strong><p>Basic arithmetic and elementary algebra are enough to start. We explain coordinate conventions as we use them and include a matrix-multiplication refresher before composition. No prior quantum physics or Qiskit experience is required.</p></aside>\n<section id=\"goals\"><h2>What questions will we be able to answer?</h2><ul><li>How can a four-setting switch use a four-component basis column and a two-bit code?</li><li>What does a probability vector say about a sealed result, and what changes when information arrives?</li><li>How can we build an operation matrix from its response to each definite input?</li><li>Why does applying A and then B give BA, and why can reversing the devices change the answer?</li></ul></section>\n<section id=\"sequence\"><h2>Follow the reasoning through four lessons</h2><ol class=\"chapter-lessons\"><li><a href=\"#classical-states\">Finite systems, states and basis labels</a><p>Start with switches and a die. Choose labels, declare their order and distinguish coordinates from binary encoding.</p></li><li><a href=\"#classical-probability\">Probability distributions and repeated observations</a><p>Open the sealed-envelope example, question a probability model, then work events, samples and conditional probabilities.</p></li><li><a href=\"#classical-operations\">Deterministic and stochastic operations</a><p>Build the four bit functions and the lecture's asymmetric random rule. Derive their action on an uncertain input.</p></li><li><a href=\"#classical-composition\">Composing operations</a><p>Follow intermediate states, combine devices and work out why their order matters.</p></li></ol></section>\n<section id=\"study\"><h2>Predict first, then calculate and compare</h2><p>When a question appears in the text, try the next step before revealing the working. In each experiment you can choose the inputs, predict the result and open the calculation. The probability sampler also lets you compare a fixed model with fresh batches. The joint-table experiment shows what changes after learning one variable.</p><p>The eight concept checks give feedback for each option. End-of-lesson exercises include worked reasoning and hints where useful. Check counts are saved in this browser as a study aid; they do not certify mastery or restrict navigation. The revised envelope and asymmetric-operation questions have their own check records.</p><p>The <a href=\"https://colab.research.google.com/github/Ashutosh-Bhatia/quantum-machine-learning/blob/main/notebooks/00-classical-information.ipynb\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Python and NumPy companion ↗</a> follows the same examples: predict, calculate by hand, run and explain. Qiskit enters with quantum circuits. You can study the written chapter fully without running the notebook.</p><p><a class=\"button\" href=\"#classical-states\">Begin Lesson 1.1</a></p></section>\n<section id=\"review\"><h2>The lecture connection and what comes next</h2><p>The narrative and recurring examples are adapted from Dr. Ashutosh Bhatia's lectures, especially 26 July, with the conditioning connection developed in the August sessions. Lesson-level source notes give recording offsets and distinguish added exposition. This is an edited teaching text, not a transcript export.</p><p>The primary slides are <cite>Lecture Slides – 1 to 7</cite>, PDF pages 3–13. The three academic figures remain drafts for instructor feedback; Figure 1 now follows the four-setting switch. Source PDFs, raw transcripts and classroom dialogue are kept outside the public website.</p><p>The route ahead remains quantum computing → classical machine learning bridge → quantum machine learning. We will revisit these classical descriptions when the rules change to amplitudes and quantum measurement, and return to the four bit functions in Deutsch's problem. Later chapters remain planned. Short earlier examples are available separately in the prototype archive.</p></section>\n"}, "lessons": [{"id": "classical-states", "number": "1.1", "title": "Finite systems, states and basis labels", "part": "Part I · Chapter 1", "status": "Transcript-led lecture notes · instructor-review draft", "objectives": ["Choose a finite state set appropriate to a modelling question.", "Translate a definite state into a labelled standard basis vector.", "Distinguish coordinate dimension from binary storage size."], "body_file": "chapters/classical-information/1-states.html", "quizzes": [{"id": "states-order", "question": "If we reorder the coordinates, does the same switch setting necessarily keep the same column vector?", "options": ["Yes: coordinate order has no effect.", "No: the coordinates can change while the labelled setting stays the same.", "No: reordering physically changes the setting."], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["The position of the 1 depends on which coordinate corresponds to the setting.", "Correct. A vector must be interpreted with its declared basis order.", "Changing our coordinate convention does not operate on the physical device."]}, {"id": "states-capacity", "question": "What is the minimum fixed-length binary storage for five distinct labels?", "options": ["Two bits", "Three bits", "Five bits"], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["Two bits provide only four distinct strings.", "Correct. Three bits provide eight strings; three are unused in a five-label code.", "Five one-hot coordinates are one representation, but they are not the minimum number of storage bits."]}], "notebook": "00-classical-information.ipynb", "notebook_kind": "classical", "source": "Lecture Slides - 1 to 7.pdf, PDF pp.3–4; 26 July 2026 lecture, 4:32 (switches, die and four settings), 17:40 (basis columns) and 50:00 (labels and indexing). The three-setting encoding extension and full worked reasoning are newly authored adaptations.", "prerequisites": "Basic arithmetic and elementary algebra. State labels, coordinate order and ket notation are explained here.", "body": "<p class=\"lead\">Before we discuss a quantum computer, let us ask a simpler question: what do we need to know about a system to describe its state? We can build the language for this question with a switch and a die, and then use it to understand computation.</p>\n<section id=\"systems\"><h2>1. Start with a system we already understand</h2>\n<p>Consider an on/off switch. If our question is simply which position has been selected, there are two possible answers: off and on. For a die resting on a table, we might ask which face is uppermost. That gives six possible answers. A switch with off, low, medium and high settings gives four.</p>\n<p>We call the collection of possible classical state labels the <strong>state set</strong>, denoted by \\(\\Sigma\\). For these examples,</p>\n\\[\\Sigma_{\\mathrm{bit}}=\\{0,1\\},\\quad \\Sigma_{\\mathrm{die}}=\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\},\\quad \\Sigma_{\\mathrm{switch}}=\\{\\mathrm{off},\\mathrm{low},\\mathrm{medium},\\mathrm{high}\\}.\\]\n<p>Notice the modelling decision we made. A real switch also has a temperature and a physical position; a die has an orientation in space. We have selected the distinctions needed for our question. If the question changes, the state description may need to change too. Describing a switch by its selected setting is enough to record that setting, but it would not be enough to predict the dynamics of a motor connected to it.</p>\n<p>We can now see a simple model of computation. A system is in some state; an operation acts on it; the system has an output state. An on/off switch is a small example. A computer memory has many more possible states, but the question is still familiar: what is the state, and how does an operation change it?</p>\n<p>Try giving a state set for another familiar device before continuing. State what your labels describe. The important first step is choosing the question, not choosing complicated mathematics.</p></section>\n<section id=\"labels\"><h2>2. Give each label a coordinate</h2>\n<p>Suppose our four-setting switch is set to medium. We could write the word “medium” and stop there. Why introduce a vector? Because we want a common mathematical language for definite states, uncertainty about states, and operations on states. A column representation will let us build that language piece by piece.</p>\n<p>Choose the coordinate order \\((\\mathrm{off},\\mathrm{low},\\mathrm{medium},\\mathrm{high})\\). Put a 1 in the coordinate corresponding to the selected label and zeros in the other coordinates:</p>\n\\[e_{\\mathrm{off}}=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\0\\\\0\\\\0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad e_{\\mathrm{low}}=\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\1\\\\0\\\\0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad e_{\\mathrm{medium}}=\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\0\\\\1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad e_{\\mathrm{high}}=\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\0\\\\0\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>This is a <strong>one-hot representation</strong>. The column for medium selects the third position in our declared list. We have not measured a new physical quantity by writing four numbers; we have represented the label in coordinates.</p>\n<h3>Pause and predict: what if we reverse the list?</h3>\n<p>Keep the switch at medium, but use the order (high, medium, low, off). Where should the 1 go now?</p>\n<details><summary>Work through the change</summary><p>Medium is the second label in the new list, so its column is \\((0,1,0,0)^{\\mathsf T}\\). The old column was \\((0,0,1,0)^{\\mathsf T}\\). Both describe medium, with different coordinate conventions. Reordering the list does not operate on the switch.</p></details>\n<p>Keep this habit throughout the course: write the order before interpreting a column. Later, the order of qubits in a circuit diagram and the order of symbols in a ket will need the same care.</p>\n<div data-quiz=\"states-order\"></div>\n<h3>Does a die have to start at label zero?</h3>\n<p>No. The physical face label 1 and a programming array position 0 are different things. We can choose the ordered face labels (1,2,3,4,5,6); the column for face 1 is then \\((1,0,0,0,0,0)^{\\mathsf T}\\). A program may store its first entry at array index 0. The mapping between label and position must be stated; it should never be guessed from the number printed on the die.</p></section>\n<section id=\"basis\"><h2>3. Basis vectors and the ket language</h2>\n<p>For a finite state set with \\(d\\) labels, we construct \\(d\\) standard coordinate vectors. Each has a single 1 in its corresponding coordinate. These form the <strong>standard basis</strong>: every \\(d\\)-component vector can be written uniquely as a weighted sum of them.</p>\n<p>Let us see what that statement means for the switch. If</p>\n\\[v=\\begin{pmatrix}v_{\\mathrm{off}}\\\\v_{\\mathrm{low}}\\\\v_{\\mathrm{medium}}\\\\v_{\\mathrm{high}}\\end{pmatrix},\\]\n<p>then</p>\n\\[v=v_{\\mathrm{off}}e_{\\mathrm{off}}+v_{\\mathrm{low}}e_{\\mathrm{low}}+v_{\\mathrm{medium}}e_{\\mathrm{medium}}+v_{\\mathrm{high}}e_{\\mathrm{high}}.\\]\n<p>Each term contributes to only one coordinate. Adding the terms reconstructs the original column. The coefficients are unique because no other basis vector contributes to that coordinate. This simple observation is why the representation is useful: we can understand a linear operation by understanding its action on the basis columns.</p>\n<p>We also write \\(|a\\rangle\\) for the basis column associated with label \\(a\\), and read it as “ket a.” For a bit, in order (0,1),</p>\n\\[|0\\rangle=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad |1\\rangle=\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>The symbols may look unfamiliar, but here they are names for columns we already understand. Using a ket does not by itself make a description quantum. In the next lesson, a weighted sum will describe classical uncertainty, and the coefficients will be probabilities. When we reach quantum states, the coefficients and the measurement rule will change. We will explain that change explicitly.</p></section>\n<section id=\"encoding\"><h2>4. Four coordinates do not mean four storage bits</h2>\n<p>How many bits do we need to record the setting of our four-setting switch? Two bits give the strings 00, 01, 10 and 11. We can assign one string to each setting: off ↦ 00, low ↦ 01, medium ↦ 10, high ↦ 11. Every setting has a different code.</p>\n<p>Compare this with the one-hot column for medium: it has four entries, but its binary label code has two digits. The coordinate representation and the storage encoding answer different questions. The column provides a basis for calculations; the binary string stores which label was selected.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/classical-information/state-representations.svg\" alt=\"The four switch settings off, low, medium and high have four-component one-hot columns and distinct two-bit codes 00, 01, 10 and 11.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 1.</strong> One selected setting, two representations. The basis order is (off, low, medium, high); the four-component column and the two-bit code have different roles. <span class=\"draft-label\">Revised draft figure for instructor review.</span></figcaption></figure>\n<p>In general, \\(n\\) bits provide \\(2^n\\) different strings: each position has two choices. To encode \\(d\\) distinct labels with fixed-length strings, we need</p>\n\\[2^n\\ge d,\\qquad n_{\\min}=\\lceil\\log_2d\\rceil.\\]\n<p>The ceiling means round up. If logarithms are unfamiliar, ask how many times we must double 1 before reaching at least \\(d\\). A one-label system needs no information-bearing bit to distinguish alternatives, since there are none.</p>\n<h3>An extension: when a code has unused strings</h3>\n<p>Now suppose a different switch has only three settings: off, low and high. One bit is insufficient because it gives only two strings. Two bits still suffice, but now we can use 00, 01 and 10 and leave 11 unused. That unused code does not automatically create another physical setting; an application must decide how to handle it.</p>\n<p>A six-state die similarly needs three fixed-length binary digits. Two digits give only four strings; three give eight, leaving two unused. None of this says that the die occupies all its possible faces at once. We have counted labels and assigned codes.</p>\n<div data-lab=\"states\"></div>\n<div data-quiz=\"states-capacity\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"practice\"><h2>5. Work it through yourself</h2>\n<p>Reading a column and constructing one yourself are different tasks. Attempt these before opening the hints or worked reasoning.</p>\n<ol class=\"exercise-list\"><li><strong>The lecture's four-setting switch.</strong> In order (off, low, medium, high), write the column and fixed binary code for medium using the code defined above. Explain why the column length differs from the code length.\n<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Find medium's position in the coordinate list, then use its assigned binary string. These are separate steps.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The column is \\((0,0,1,0)^{\\mathsf T}\\); the code is 10. Four basis coordinates provide a vector representation of the four labels, while two binary digits provide four different strings for storing a label.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Reorder without operating on the device.</strong> Rewrite the medium column in order (high, medium, low, off). Does the fixed code 10 change?\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The column becomes \\((0,1,0,0)^{\\mathsf T}\\). The fixed code remains 10 because we changed the coordinate list, not the label-to-code assignment or the switch setting. A new code assignment would be a separate convention change.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Face label or array position?</strong> For face order (1,2,3,4,5,6), write the column for face 3. Which entry would a zero-indexed array select?\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The column is \\((0,0,1,0,0,0)^{\\mathsf T}\\). Face 3 occupies the third list position, which is array index 2. A numerical face label need not equal its storage index.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Five messages.</strong> Find the minimum fixed-length binary storage, give a possible code, and count unused strings.\n<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Count the strings available with two digits and then with three.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>Three bits suffice: two give only four strings, while three give eight. Use 000, 001, 010, 011 and 100, leaving three strings unused. A one-hot representation would instead have five coordinates.</p></details></li></ol>\n<aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>What have we built?</strong><p>Choose the state labels, declare their order, and represent each definite label by a basis column. We now have a language for calculations. Next we ask how to use that same language when the system has a definite classical result but we do not know it.</p></aside></section>\n"}, {"id": "classical-probability", "number": "1.2", "title": "Probability distributions and repeated observations", "part": "Part I · Chapter 1", "status": "Transcript-led lecture notes · instructor-review draft", "objectives": ["Validate a probability vector and calculate event probabilities.", "Separate a model probability from a finite empirical frequency.", "Calculate conditional probabilities from events and joint tables, including zero-probability boundaries."], "body_file": "chapters/classical-information/2-probability.html", "quizzes": [{"id": "prob-envelope", "question": "The envelope contains a recorded result, with pass labelled 0 and fail labelled 1. What does p=(3/4,1/4)ᵀ describe before opening it?", "options": ["Our probabilities for the two recorded labels.", "A coherent quantum superposition of pass and fail.", "A guarantee that opening four envelopes produces exactly one failure."], "answer": 0, "feedback": ["Correct. It describes uncertainty about the classical label. Observing pass updates our information to (1,0)ᵀ without requiring the printed result to change.", "The ket/column notation alone does not make a classical letter a quantum system. The entries here are probabilities, not amplitudes.", "Expected proportions are not a guarantee about a finite batch. A batch also requires a specified repeated-preparation model."]}, {"id": "prob-frequency", "question": "A model has P(1)=0.25, but a 100-trial batch contains 28 ones. What follows?", "options": ["The exact probability must be 0.28.", "The observed frequency is 0.28; it can differ from the model probability.", "The model has been proved false."], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["A finite frequency is an estimate, not a proof of the exact probability.", "Correct. Interpreting the size of the discrepancy also requires the sampling assumptions and an uncertainty analysis.", "This observation alone does not prove the model false. Finite samples fluctuate."]}], "notebook": "00-classical-information.ipynb", "notebook_kind": "classical", "source": "Lecture Slides - 1 to 7.pdf, PDF pp.5–6; 26 July 2026 lecture, 11:02 (changing-team/model assumptions), 20:49–23:55 (sealed envelope and probability coefficients); 16 August, 35:58–48:29, and 30 August, 24:36 (conditioning and normalization). The complete joint table, event calculations and indicator derivation are newly authored supporting exposition.", "prerequisites": "Lesson 1.1: ordered state labels, basis columns and their meaning.", "body": "<p class=\"lead\">Suppose a sealed envelope contains a result: pass or fail. The result has been recorded, but we have not opened the envelope. How can we describe what we know about it? This question takes us from a definite label to a probability vector.</p>\n<section id=\"distributions\"><h2>1. The envelope and our uncertainty</h2>\n<p>Let us label pass by 0 and fail by 1. Suppose our information is represented by probabilities 3/4 for pass and 1/4 for fail. In the coordinate order (0,1), we write</p>\n\\[p=\\frac34|0\\rangle+\\frac14|1\\rangle=\\begin{pmatrix}3/4\\\\1/4\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>What are these coefficients saying? They describe our uncertainty about the label inside the envelope. They do not say that the printed result is physically both pass and fail. If we open the envelope and find pass, our updated description is \\((1,0)^{\\mathsf T}\\). We have learned the label. Opening the envelope need not have changed the result written on the letter.</p>\n<p>A probability vector can also describe a random preparation that produces different labels on different trials. In either case, we must state what the random variable and its probabilities refer to. An unopened fixed letter and a procedure for preparing many letters are different experiments, even if we use the same numerical column.</p>\n<h3>The general definition</h3>\n<p>For ordered state labels \\((a_1,\\ldots,a_d)\\), let \\(p_j=\\Pr(X=a_j)\\). A complete distribution is a column satisfying</p>\n\\[p=\\begin{pmatrix}p_1\\\\\\vdots\\\\p_d\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad p_j\\ge0,\\qquad \\sum_{j=1}^{d}p_j=1.\\]\n<p>The entries are nonnegative probabilities. Their sum is one because the labels describe mutually exclusive, exhaustive outcomes: precisely one of the labels applies on a trial. Thus \\((0.2,0.3)^{\\mathsf T}\\) is incomplete on a two-state set, while \\((1.2,-0.2)^{\\mathsf T}\\) is invalid despite summing to one.</p>\n<p>A <strong>point mass</strong> records certainty about one label. Our basis columns \\((1,0)^{\\mathsf T}\\) and \\((0,1)^{\\mathsf T}\\) are the bit examples. Intermediate nonnegative weights describe uncertainty. In this chapter the coefficients themselves are probabilities; we do not square them. Quantum amplitudes and their measurement rule will be introduced later.</p>\n<div data-quiz=\"prob-envelope\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"events\"><h2>2. Ask a question about several outcomes</h2>\n<p>An <strong>event</strong> is a subset of the state set. Return to a die, now with distribution in face order 1 through 6:</p>\n\\[p=\\frac1{12}\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\2\\\\1\\\\3\\\\2\\\\3\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>Before calculating, identify which labels answer the question “is the upper face even?” They are \\(E=\\{2,4,6\\}\\). Only one face is observed on a trial, so the routes are mutually exclusive and we add:</p>\n\\[\\Pr(E)=p_2+p_4+p_6=\\frac{2+3+3}{12}=\\frac23.\\]\n<p>We have not created a new physical face called “even.” We have asked a question that groups existing outcomes. The complement \\(E^c\\) contains the remaining labels, so \\(\\Pr(E^c)=1-\\Pr(E)\\).</p>\n<p>When two events overlap, simply adding their probabilities counts the intersection twice. The correct identity is</p>\n\\[\\Pr(A\\cup B)=\\Pr(A)+\\Pr(B)-\\Pr(A\\cap B).\\]\n<p>For disjoint events the intersection term vanishes. This is the condition that made the die-face calculation above valid.</p></section>\n<section id=\"frequency\"><h2>3. Where did the probabilities come from?</h2>\n<p>A model can specify probabilities, and observations can help us estimate them. But assigning a probability deserves care. Suppose a team won seven of its last ten matches. Does that make its probability of winning the next match exactly 0.7? What if strong players have retired, or the opposing team has changed?</p>\n<p>The historical frequency is a fact about those ten matches. Using it as the next-match probability introduces assumptions about how relevant those observations are. We should ask what conditions stayed the same and what changed. This is already a familiar concern in machine learning: a numerical estimate does not remove the need for a faithful model of the data.</p>\n<p>For a simpler calculation, assume a fixed bit-preparation model with \\(\\Pr(X=1)=1/4\\). Repeat that preparation independently \\(N\\) times under unchanged conditions. If \\(N_1\\) trials produce 1, the observed frequency is</p>\n\\[\\widehat p_1=\\frac{N_1}{N}.\\]\n<p>The hat distinguishes the finite-sample estimate from the model probability. With 100 trials the expected count is 25 ones. A batch might instead have 28, giving \\(\\widehat p_1=0.28\\). That observation does not prove that the exact probability is 0.28.</p>\n<h3>Work out the expected count</h3>\n<p>Let \\(I_t\\) be 1 when trial \\(t\\) produces a 1, and 0 otherwise. Then \\(\\mathbb E[I_t]=1\\cdot p_1+0\\cdot(1-p_1)=p_1\\). Since \\(N_1=I_1+\\cdots+I_N\\), linearity of expectation gives</p>\n\\[\\mathbb E[N_1]=\\sum_{t=1}^{N}\\mathbb E[I_t]=Np_1,\\qquad \\mathbb E[\\widehat p_1]=p_1.\\]\n<p>Equal marginal probabilities suffice for this expectation calculation; independence matters for the usual variance and concentration results developed later. Our sampler assumes independent fresh trials of the same model. A drifting preparation or dependent trials may require another model.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/classical-information/probability-frequency.svg\" alt=\"Exact model probabilities 0.75 and 0.25 are compared with illustrative counts 72 and 28 in a batch of 100 trials.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 2.</strong> The model and one possible observed batch answer different questions. Counts 72 and 28 are an illustration, not a reported experiment or a guaranteed result. <span class=\"draft-label\">Draft figure for instructor review.</span></figcaption></figure>\n<div data-lab=\"probability\"></div>\n<div data-quiz=\"prob-frequency\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"conditioning\"><h2>4. When information arrives, normalize again</h2>\n<p>Suppose we learn that the weighted die's result is even. Which outcomes survive, and what should their probabilities add up to now? The surviving faces are 2, 4 and 6, in the ratio 2:3:3. Their original total probability is \\(8/12\\). Divide each surviving probability by that total:</p>\n\\[\\Pr(X=j\\mid E)=\\frac{\\Pr(\\{X=j\\}\\cap E)}{\\Pr(E)}=\\begin{cases}p_j/\\Pr(E),&j\\in E,\\\\0,&j\\notin E,\\end{cases}\\qquad \\Pr(E)>0.\\]\n<p>Our conditional distribution in full face order is therefore</p>\n\\[p_{\\mid E}=\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\1/4\\\\0\\\\3/8\\\\0\\\\3/8\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>Check the result: \\(1/4+3/8+3/8=1\\). We have restricted the possibilities and rescaled the remaining weights. If we had only deleted the odd outcomes, the entries would still sum to 2/3, not one.</p>\n<h3>Two variables: information about one changes the description of the other</h3>\n<p>We will need this reasoning when we discuss two qubits, so let us first work a completely classical example. Let \\(X,Y\\) each take labels 0 and 1. The table contains <em>joint probabilities</em> \\(\\Pr(X=x,Y=y)\\), with rows indexed by X and columns by Y.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><caption>A joint classical distribution; all four cells sum to one</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">X label</th><th scope=\"col\">Y=0</th><th scope=\"col\">Y=1</th><th scope=\"col\">Row total P(X)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">X=0</th><td>1/12</td><td>1/4</td><td>1/3</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">X=1</th><td>1/3</td><td>1/3</td><td>2/3</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>First ignore X. Summing down each column gives \\(\\Pr(Y=0)=5/12\\) and \\(\\Pr(Y=1)=7/12\\). Now suppose we learn that X=0. We retain only the first row. Its total is \\(1/12+1/4=1/3\\), so</p>\n\\[\\Pr(Y=0\\mid X=0)=\\frac{1/12}{1/3}=\\frac14,\\qquad \\Pr(Y=1\\mid X=0)=\\frac{1/4}{1/3}=\\frac34.\\]\n<p>The probabilities for Y have changed because the new information selects a particular part of the joint distribution. This example is not independent: the conditional distribution differs from the marginal distribution. If X and Y were independent, learning X would leave Y's distribution unchanged. Try both cases below.</p>\n<div data-lab=\"conditioning\"></div>\n<p>What if the selected event has probability zero? This elementary conditional formula is undefined. Dividing by zero cannot create a conditional distribution; an observed event excluded by our model calls for reconsidering that model.</p>\n<p>The table above contains probabilities, so we divide by an event probability. Later we will group quantum <em>amplitudes</em> by a measured label and divide the surviving amplitude vector by the square root of the outcome probability. We will derive that step from normalization rather than memorize a change of denominator.</p>\n<aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>Keep the envelope's limitation in view.</strong><p>Classical conditioning updates our information. It does not by itself describe a physical disturbance or demonstrate quantum coherence. The analogy helps us understand restricted possibilities and normalization; quantum measurement will need its own precise rules.</p></aside></section>\n<section id=\"practice\"><h2>5. Work it through yourself</h2>\n<ol class=\"exercise-list\"><li><strong>The recorded result.</strong> With pass ↦ 0 and fail ↦ 1, what does \\((3/4,1/4)^{\\mathsf T}\\) mean before opening the envelope? What is our updated column after observing fail?\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The entries describe our probabilities for the two recorded labels. Observing fail gives the point mass \\((0,1)^{\\mathsf T}\\). This is an information update, not a claim that opening the envelope changed the printed result or that the letter was a coherent quantum state.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Validity and an event.</strong> Is \\((0.1,0.4,0.5)^{\\mathsf T}\\) a valid distribution on (A,B,C)? Find the probability of not B.\n<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Check nonnegativity and the total before adding the probabilities of the relevant outcomes.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The entries are nonnegative and sum to one. Not B contains A and C, giving \\(0.1+0.5=0.6\\), equivalently \\(1-0.4\\).</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Count or model?</strong> A fixed model has \\(p_1=0.3\\). In 200 independent trials we observe 68 ones. Calculate the expected count and observed frequency. Does this prove that the model is wrong?\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The expectation is \\(200(0.3)=60\\); the frequency is \\(68/200=0.34\\). This finite observation alone does not establish an exact probability or prove the model false. Assessing a discrepancy requires an uncertainty analysis under the sampling assumptions. If conditions changed, we should also reconsider whether the fixed model is appropriate.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Condition the die.</strong> Condition on faces \\(A=\\{1,2,3\\}\\). Find \\(\\Pr(X=2\\mid A)\\). What happens for the empty event?\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>\\(\\Pr(A)=4/12\\), so \\(\\Pr(X=2\\mid A)=(2/12)/(4/12)=1/2\\). The empty event has probability zero and the elementary conditional formula is undefined.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Return to the joint table.</strong> Given X=1, calculate the conditional distribution of Y. Compare it with Y's marginal distribution.\n<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Select the X=1 row and divide by its row total, not by the total of the whole table.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The selected row is \\((1/3,1/3)\\) with total 2/3. Its normalized distribution is \\((1/2,1/2)^{\\mathsf T}\\), different from the marginal \\((5/12,7/12)^{\\mathsf T}\\). Learning X gives information about Y in this model.</p></details></li></ol>\n<aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>What have we added?</strong><p>A column can describe certainty, uncertainty or random preparation, provided its meaning is stated. We can calculate events, compare a model with finite samples, and update a distribution when information arrives. Next we ask how to describe an operation acting on such a column.</p></aside></section>\n"}, {"id": "classical-operations", "number": "1.3", "title": "Deterministic and stochastic operations", "part": "Part I · Chapter 1", "status": "Transcript-led lecture notes · instructor-review draft", "objectives": ["Build a matrix from its response to definite inputs.", "Derive the distribution update p′=Mp and prove normalization preservation.", "Distinguish stochastic validity, invertibility, and allowed reversal."], "body_file": "chapters/classical-information/3-operations.html", "quizzes": [{"id": "operations-asymmetric", "question": "An operation keeps input 0 unchanged and sends input 1 to 0 or 1 equally. In output order (0,1), which column represents input 1?", "options": ["(1/2,1/2)ᵀ", "(1,0)ᵀ", "(3/4,1/4)ᵀ"], "answer": 0, "feedback": ["Correct. Column 1 is the output distribution given input 1. Column 0 is (1,0)ᵀ.", "That is the output column for input 0, which the rule keeps unchanged.", "Those are the weights of a separate uncertain input preparation used in the lesson. They do not define this operation’s response to definite input 1."]}, {"id": "operations-inverse", "question": "Reset-to-zero preserves total probability. Must it therefore be reversible for every input?", "options": ["Yes: preservation of probability guarantees an inverse.", "No: distinct inputs are mapped to the same output.", "Only if the input distribution is uniform."], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["Probability preservation is weaker than reversibility. Reset is a counterexample.", "Correct. The common output cannot identify which of the distinct inputs occurred.", "A chosen input distribution does not create an inverse that recovers every input."]}], "notebook": "00-classical-information.ipynb", "notebook_kind": "classical", "source": "Lecture Slides - 1 to 7.pdf, PDF pp.7,11; 26 July 2026 lecture, 26:34–35:41 (four functions and matrix representations), 1:07:05 (keep 0; randomize input 1 equally) and 1:16:27 (weighted deterministic actions). The exact decomposition A=(I+R0)/2, total-probability proof and inverse examples provide checked supporting exposition.", "prerequisites": "Lessons 1.1–1.2: basis columns, probability distributions and conditional probabilities.", "body": "<p class=\"lead\">We can now describe a definite bit and uncertainty about a bit. What happens when we apply an operation? Let us begin by writing down what it does to each possible input. The matrix will follow from that rule.</p>\n<section id=\"deterministic\"><h2>1. Deterministic operations as functions</h2>\n<p>A deterministic operation on a finite state set is a function \\(f:\\Sigma\\to\\Sigma\\): for every definite input label, it specifies one definite output label. “Deterministic” describes the operation's response given its input. It does not require the input to be known to us.</p>\n<p>How many different deterministic rules can we define for one input bit and one output bit? Try listing them before reading the table. There are two choices for \\(f(0)\\) and, independently, two choices for \\(f(1)\\), giving \\(2\\times2=4\\) functions.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><caption>All deterministic one-bit functions</caption><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>f(0)</th><th>f(1)</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Identity</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>Keep the input</td></tr><tr><td>Flip</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>Exchange 0 and 1</td></tr><tr><td>Reset to 0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>Output 0 for either input</td></tr><tr><td>Reset to 1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>Output 1 for either input</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>We could draw four input-to-output mapping diagrams. Instead, let us use the columns we have already built. For each definite input, put its output column in the corresponding position of a matrix. In symbols,</p>\n\\[M_{ij}=\\begin{cases}1,&i=f(j),\\\\0,&i\\ne f(j).\\end{cases}\\]\n<p>The <strong>column index is the input</strong> and the row index is the output. Column \\(j\\) is the output basis vector produced by input \\(|j\\rangle\\). Therefore \\(M|j\\rangle=|f(j)\\rangle\\). This rule is safer than memorizing matrices without knowing which direction their indices represent.</p>\n<p>In basis order \\((0,1)\\), the four matrices are</p>\n\\[I=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix},\\quad X=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad R_0=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad R_1=\\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>Test this rule on the two basis inputs yourself. For example, both columns of \\(R_0\\) are \\((1,0)^{\\mathsf T}\\), because either input is sent to 0. Multiplying by \\(|0\\rangle\\) selects the first column; multiplying by \\(|1\\rangle\\) selects the second. We have translated a rule in words into a calculation.</p><p>Remember these four functions. Later we will meet them again in Deutsch’s problem, where the question is about a property of an unknown function. Here we first understand what each function does. The symbol \\(X\\) denotes the classical flip matrix; the same numerical matrix will later act on quantum amplitudes, under a different state model.</p></section>\n<section id=\"stochastic\"><h2>2. An operation can behave probabilistically</h2>\n<p>Now consider a different rule: if the input is 0, do nothing; if the input is 1, output 0 or 1 with equal probabilities. The input can be definite even though the operation's response is random.</p>\n<h3>Predict each output before building the matrix</h3>\n<p>On input 0, the output distribution is \\((1,0)^{\\mathsf T}\\). On input 1, it is \\((1/2,1/2)^{\\mathsf T}\\). Put these in input order (0,1):</p>\n\\[A=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1/2\\\\0&1/2\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>Try applying A to both basis columns. The results should reproduce the rule we started with:</p>\n\\[A|0\\rangle=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad A|1\\rangle=\\begin{pmatrix}1/2\\\\1/2\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>This is why reading a matrix column by column is useful. The first column says what happens given input 0; the second says what happens given input 1. We have not inferred an input probability from an output.</p>\n<p>For a general random operation, write</p>\n\\[M_{ij}=\\Pr(\\text{output}=i\\mid\\text{input}=j).\\]\n<p>For each fixed input j, its possible outputs form a complete probability distribution. Consequently,</p>\n\\[M_{ij}\\ge0,\\qquad \\sum_iM_{ij}=1\\quad\\text{for every column }j.\\]\n<p>This defines a <strong>column-stochastic matrix</strong>. The rows of A sum to 3/2 and 1/2, which is fine: the columns are the input-conditioned distributions. Texts using row vectors may use a row-stochastic convention. We will consistently use columns.</p>\n<div data-quiz=\"operations-asymmetric\"></div>\n<h3>Build the random rule from simpler actions</h3>\n<p>We can understand the same operation another way. Choose, with equal probabilities, between doing nothing and resetting to 0. If the input is 0, either choice leaves it 0. If the input is 1, identity leaves it 1 and reset sends it to 0. Thus</p>\n\\[A=\\frac12I+\\frac12R_0=\\frac12\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&1\\end{pmatrix}+\\frac12\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1/2\\\\0&1/2\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>The weights describe a random choice between two deterministic actions. We add the weighted matrices because the alternative actions are selected with those probabilities; this is different from performing one after the other.</p>\n<h3>Compare with a symmetric random flip</h3>\n<p>Suppose instead that we flip either input with probability \\(\\varepsilon\\) and keep it with probability \\(1-\\varepsilon\\), where \\(0\\le\\varepsilon\\le1\\). Then</p>\n\\[B_\\varepsilon=(1-\\varepsilon)I+\\varepsilon X=\\begin{pmatrix}1-\\varepsilon&\\varepsilon\\\\\\varepsilon&1-\\varepsilon\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>This rule treats the two inputs symmetrically; A does not. At \\(\\varepsilon=0\\) we have identity; at 1 a definite flip; at 1/2 either definite input produces a uniform output. Compare A and \\(B_{1/2}\\) on input 0 to see why they are different operations.</p></section>\n<section id=\"evolution\"><h2>3. What if we do not know the input?</h2>\n<p>So far we tested definite inputs. Now suppose the preparation is described by p. Should we apply the matrix only to the most likely label? No: each possible input contributes to the output, weighted by its probability.</p>\n<p>Let \\(p_j\\) be the probability of input j. Output i can be reached through any input j. The probability for each route is the input probability times the conditional transition probability. Summing mutually exclusive input routes gives the law of total probability:</p>\n\\[p'_i=\\sum_j\\Pr(\\text{output}=i\\mid\\text{input}=j)\\Pr(\\text{input}=j)=\\sum_jM_{ij}p_j.\\]\n<p>This is precisely the ith entry of a matrix–vector product. Therefore,</p>\n\\[p'=Mp,\\qquad \\begin{pmatrix}p'_0\\\\p'_1\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}M_{00}p_0+M_{01}p_1\\\\M_{10}p_0+M_{11}p_1\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<h3>Work the lecture's asymmetric operation on an uncertain input</h3>\n<p>Use the familiar input \\(p=(3/4,1/4)^{\\mathsf T}\\). Predict the two output probabilities, then calculate:</p>\n\\[Ap=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1/2\\\\0&1/2\\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix}3/4\\\\1/4\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}3/4+(1/2)(1/4)\\\\(1/2)(1/4)\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}7/8\\\\1/8\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>Why 7/8 for output 0? All the probability already on input 0 stays there, and half the probability on input 1 also arrives at 0. For output 1, only the input-1 route contributes, and only half of it survives. The multiplication records those routes.</p>\n<p>As a comparison, \\(B_{1/4}\\) acting on the same p gives</p>\n\\[B_{1/4}p=\\begin{pmatrix}9/16+1/16\\\\3/16+3/16\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}5/8\\\\3/8\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>The different results follow from different transition rules, not a change in the input. Check both rules in the experiment after making your own prediction.</p>\n<div data-lab=\"operations\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"normalization\"><h2>4. Why the output is still a probability distribution</h2>\n<p>We should verify that the update rule respects the definition of a distribution. Each output entry is a sum of products of nonnegative numbers, so \\(p'_i\\ge0\\). For the total probability, interchange the finite sums:</p>\n\\[\\begin{aligned}\\sum_i p'_i&=\\sum_i\\sum_jM_{ij}p_j\\\\&=\\sum_jp_j\\left(\\sum_iM_{ij}\\right)\\\\&=\\sum_jp_j=1.\\end{aligned}\\]\n<p>The third line uses the fact that each column of \\(M\\) sums to one. Thus every valid column-stochastic matrix maps every valid input distribution to another valid distribution. This proof explains why the column condition is the right one; checking a few numerical inputs alone would not establish the general statement.</p>\n<p>A deterministic function is a special stochastic operation: each column has one 1 and all other entries 0. It still acts on uncertain input distributions by the same rule. There are therefore two distinct sources of uncertainty: uncertainty in the input preparation and randomness in the operation. They can occur separately or together.</p></section>\n<section id=\"reversibility\"><h2>5. Preserving probability does not imply reversibility</h2>\n<p>If both inputs become 0, can we recover the input from that output? This question separates probability preservation from reversibility. The flip can be undone: applying it twice restores every input, so \\(X^{-1}=X\\). Identity is its own inverse too. Reset-to-zero sends both input labels to the same output, so the output cannot tell us which input occurred.</p>\n<p>There cannot be an inverse operation recovering every reset input, because it would have to send the same vector \\(|0\\rangle\\) to two different vectors. The identical columns of \\(R_0\\) make that loss of input information visible. Reset nevertheless preserves total probability perfectly.</p>\n<p>An invertible numerical matrix need not have an inverse that is a physical stochastic operation. For example,</p>\n\\[B_{1/4}^{-1}=\\begin{pmatrix}3/2&-1/2\\\\-1/2&3/2\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>This is an algebraic inverse, but it contains negative entries. It cannot act on every probability distribution as a stochastic process. Deterministic bijections, represented by permutation matrices, do have stochastic inverses. This distinction prepares us for the stronger restrictions on closed-system quantum operations later.</p>\n<div data-quiz=\"operations-inverse\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"practice\"><h2>6. Work it through yourself</h2><p>Start by specifying the input-conditioned columns. Then calculate and explain the result; an answer without the interpretation leaves half the task unfinished.</p>\n<ol class=\"exercise-list\"><li><strong>A random choice or a sequence?</strong> Explain why choosing identity or reset-to-zero with equal probabilities gives A, but applying identity and then reset-to-zero does not.<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>A random choice gives \\((I+R_0)/2=A\\), with input 1 producing a half/half distribution. A sequence gives \\(R_0I=R_0\\), which always outputs 0. Addition with probabilistic weights and temporal composition describe different experiments.</p></details></li><li><strong>Build a matrix from words.</strong> An operation leaves 0 unchanged. Given 1, it outputs 0 with probability 1/3 and 1 with probability 2/3. Construct its matrix and apply it to \\(p=(1/4,3/4)^{\\mathsf T}\\).\n<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>Fill the first column from input 0 and the second from input 1. Then multiply by the input probabilities.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The matrix is \\(M=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1/3\\\\0&2/3\\end{pmatrix}\\). Therefore \\(Mp=(1/4+(1/3)(3/4),\\ (2/3)(3/4))^{\\mathsf T}=(1/2,1/2)^{\\mathsf T}\\). Both columns sum to one, and the result is normalized.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Check the convention.</strong> Is \\(C=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\1/2&1/2\\end{pmatrix}\\) a valid operation on our column probability vectors?\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>No. The column sums are 3/2 and 1/2. For example, \\(C|0\\rangle=(1,1/2)^{\\mathsf T}\\) is not a normalized distribution. Its rows summing to one does not make it valid under our column convention.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Reversal.</strong> Explain why reset cannot be reversed for every unknown input. Is the algebraic inverse of \\(B_{1/4}\\) a stochastic matrix?\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>Reset maps distinct inputs to the same output, so no function of that output can recover both inputs correctly. The stated inverse of \\(B_{1/4}\\) reverses the matrix algebra but has negative entries, so it is not a stochastic operation on arbitrary distributions.</p></details></li></ol>\n<aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>What have we learned about an operation?</strong><p>Begin with its rule on each definite input. Those output columns build the matrix, and total probability gives \\(p'=Mp\\). Check normalization, then ask separately whether information about the input can be recovered. Next we connect operations in sequence.</p></aside></section>\n"}, {"id": "classical-composition", "number": "1.4", "title": "Composing operations", "part": "Part I · Chapter 1", "status": "Transcript-led lecture notes · instructor-review draft", "objectives": ["Translate a temporal sequence into the correct matrix product.", "Show a noncommuting pair and distinguish it from associativity.", "Prove that a compatible product of stochastic matrices is stochastic."], "body_file": "chapters/classical-information/4-composition.html", "quizzes": [{"id": "composition-order", "question": "A acts first, then B, on a column probability vector p. Which expression describes the result?", "options": ["ABp", "BAp", "A+B+p"], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["ABp applies B to p first; it reverses the stated sequence.", "Correct. The intermediate state is Ap, so the second action is B(Ap).", "A temporal composition is matrix multiplication, not addition."]}, {"id": "composition-commute", "question": "Identity and flip agree on the uniform bit distribution. Are they therefore the same operation?", "options": ["Yes: one matching output proves equality.", "No: they disagree on a definite basis input.", "They agree on every other input too."], "answer": 1, "feedback": ["One input is not enough to establish equality of linear maps.", "Correct. On |0⟩, identity outputs |0⟩ while flip outputs |1⟩.", "They agree on the uniform vector, but not on the basis inputs."]}], "notebook": "00-classical-information.ipynb", "notebook_kind": "classical", "source": "Lecture Slides - 1 to 7.pdf, PDF pp.12–13; 26 July 2026 lecture, 1:32:56–1:35:38 (function composition, combined operators and order); 30 August, 1:39:47 (the later H→S→H→T circuit analogy). The matrix refresher, reset/flip proof, asymmetric two-stage exercise, closure proof and state-sufficiency discussion expand the lecture material.", "prerequisites": "Lesson 1.3: operation columns and p′=Mp. The matrix-multiplication refresher below supplies the required arithmetic.", "body": "<p class=\"lead\">Suppose we connect two small devices: the first changes a bit, and the second acts on its output. Can we replace the pair by one operation? Let us follow the intermediate state first, then see why a matrix product describes the whole process.</p>\n<section id=\"refresher\"><h2>1. A short matrix-multiplication refresher</h2>\n<p>We already have state columns and operation matrices. Before combining devices, let us check the multiplication itself. A matrix–vector product takes one row at a time, multiplies its entries by the matching vector entries, and adds the results. For</p>\n\\[A=\\begin{pmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad v=\\begin{pmatrix}u\\\\w\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad Av=\\begin{pmatrix}au+bw\\\\cu+dw\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>A matrix–matrix product applies the same rule to each column of the right-hand matrix. If</p>\n\\[B=\\begin{pmatrix}e&f\\\\g&h\\end{pmatrix},\\]\n<p>then</p>\n\\[AB=\\begin{pmatrix}ae+bg&af+bh\\\\ce+dg&cf+dh\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>This is not entry-by-entry multiplication. The \\((i,j)\\) entry is \\((AB)_{ij}=\\sum_k A_{ik}B_{kj}\\). If \\(B\\) maps input states to intermediate states and \\(A\\) maps intermediate states to output states, this sum follows every possible intermediate route.</p>\n<p>Dimensions are part of the meaning. If \\(B\\) maps a \\(d\\)-state input to an \\(r\\)-state intermediate system and \\(A\\) maps that to an \\(m\\)-state output, their dimensions are \\(r\\times d\\) and \\(m\\times r\\). The product \\(AB\\) is \\(m\\times d\\). Its inner dimensions match because the two operations share the same intermediate labels and order. Our lab uses two-state systems, but the rule is more general.</p></section>\n<section id=\"order\"><h2>2. Why the rightmost matrix acts first</h2>\n<p>Think first about ordinary functions. If we apply f and then g, we write \\(g(f(x))\\): f acts on the input before g. For our devices, start with a column \\(p\\). Apply \\(A\\), obtaining the intermediate state \\(p^{(1)}=Ap\\). The second device B receives that result:</p>\n\\[p^{(2)}=Bp^{(1)}=B(Ap)=(BA)p.\\]\n<p>The last equality uses <strong>associativity</strong>: compatible matrix multiplication can be regrouped without changing its value. It does not permit us to interchange the factors. The operation closest to the input column acts first. Thus the temporal instruction “A, then B” corresponds to \\(BA\\), not \\(AB\\).</p>\n<p>For an ordered sequence \\(M_1,M_2,\\ldots,M_n\\),</p>\n\\[p^{(n)}=M_nM_{n-1}\\cdots M_2M_1p.\\]\n<p>There is no conflict between a process diagram read left to right and a product acting right to left. They are two notations for the same sequence. Write an intermediate state whenever the shorthand becomes confusing.</p>\n<div data-quiz=\"composition-order\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"counterexample\"><h2>3. Worked example: reset and flip do not commute</h2>\n<p>Use the reset-to-zero and flip matrices</p>\n\\[R_0=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad X=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>Before looking at a product, predict each stage. If we reset a bit to 0 and then flip it, what must the final label be? If we reverse the devices, what changes? Start with any normalized bit distribution \\(p=(p_0,p_1)^{\\mathsf T}\\). Reset first:</p>\n\\[R_0p=\\begin{pmatrix}p_0+p_1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}.\\]\n<p>Flipping that result gives \\(X(R_0p)=(0,1)^{\\mathsf T}\\): the output is certainly 1. The combined matrix confirms this:</p>\n\\[XR_0=\\begin{pmatrix}0&0\\\\1&1\\end{pmatrix}=R_1.\\]\n<p>Reverse the physical order. Flipping the input first exchanges its probabilities, but resetting afterwards always gives 0:</p>\n\\[R_0(Xp)=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad R_0X=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&0\\end{pmatrix}=R_0.\\]\n<p>Consequently \\(XR_0\\ne R_0X\\). These operations <strong>do not commute</strong>. The difference is not a rounding error or a matter of which input was most probable: the two output distributions are different for every normalized input.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"figure-canvas\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Academic figure; scroll horizontally on a narrow screen\"><img src=\"figures/classical-information/composition-order.svg\" alt=\"Reset to zero followed by flip always outputs one and has product X R0. Flip followed by reset always outputs zero and has product R0 X.\" loading=\"lazy\"></div><figcaption><strong>Figure 3.</strong> Temporal order and matrix order describe the same calculation. Associativity allows regrouping; it does not allow exchanging reset and flip. <span class=\"draft-label\">Draft figure for instructor review.</span></figcaption></figure>\n<div data-lab=\"composition\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"commuting\"><h2>4. Some operations do commute</h2>\n<p>“Matrix multiplication is not commutative” means we cannot assume \\(AB=BA\\) for arbitrary matrices. It does not mean no pair ever commutes. Identity commutes with every compatible square matrix. Any matrix commutes with itself. Less trivially, a random-flip matrix \\(B_\\varepsilon=(1-\\varepsilon)I+\\varepsilon X\\) commutes with \\(X\\):</p>\n\\[\\begin{aligned}XB_\\varepsilon&=(1-\\varepsilon)X+\\varepsilon X^2=(1-\\varepsilon)X+\\varepsilon I,\\\\B_\\varepsilon X&=(1-\\varepsilon)X+\\varepsilon X^2=(1-\\varepsilon)X+\\varepsilon I.\\end{aligned}\\]\n<p>We used \\(X^2=I\\), which simply says that two definite bit flips undo one another. Equality of outputs for one chosen input, however, is not enough to prove equality of two operation matrices. They might agree on that input and disagree on another. Equality as linear maps can be checked on an entire basis, since every vector is a linear combination of basis vectors.</p>\n<div data-quiz=\"composition-commute\"></div></section>\n<section id=\"closure\"><h2>5. A composition is still a valid stochastic operation</h2>\n<p>Let \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) be compatible column-stochastic matrices. The combined operation is \\(C=BA\\), whose entries are</p>\n\\[C_{ij}=\\sum_k B_{ik}A_{kj}.\\]\n<p>Every entry is nonnegative because each term is a product of nonnegative entries. For a fixed input column \\(j\\),</p>\n\\[\\begin{aligned}\\sum_i C_{ij}&=\\sum_i\\sum_k B_{ik}A_{kj}\\\\&=\\sum_k A_{kj}\\left(\\sum_iB_{ik}\\right)\\\\&=\\sum_k A_{kj}=1.\\end{aligned}\\]\n<p>The penultimate equality uses the column sum of \\(B\\); the final equality uses the column sum of \\(A\\). Therefore \\(BA\\) is column-stochastic. By applying this argument repeatedly, any finite sequence of valid stochastic operations remains a valid stochastic operation.</p>\n<p>The probabilistic interpretation is also useful. The index \\(k\\) ranges over the intermediate state. The first operation sends input \\(j\\) to \\(k\\) with probability \\(A_{kj}\\), and the second sends \\(k\\) to output \\(i\\) with probability \\(B_{ik}\\). Multiplying the route probabilities and summing over intermediate labels gives the composite transition rule.</p>\n<aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>A modelling assumption behind the product</strong><p>The same second-operation rule \\(B\\) must apply given the intermediate state. If the second device also depends on hidden history, shared randomness, or a changing environment not represented in that state, the state description may need to be enlarged. A convenient matrix product does not eliminate the need for a faithful model.</p></aside></section>\n<section id=\"practice\"><h2>6. Work it through yourself</h2><p>Write the input, the intermediate state and the output before simplifying the combined matrix. Then check that both calculation routes agree.</p>\n<ol class=\"exercise-list\"><li><strong>Return to the asymmetric rule.</strong> Begin with \\(p=(3/4,1/4)^{\\mathsf T}\\). Apply the previous lesson’s operation A that keeps 0 and randomizes input 1 equally, then apply a definite flip X. Find the intermediate vector, final vector, and combined matrix.\n<details><summary>Hint</summary><p>The intermediate result is Ap. The second operation gives X(Ap), so the combined matrix is XA.</p></details>\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The intermediate result is \\((7/8,1/8)^{\\mathsf T}\\). Flipping gives \\((1/8,7/8)^{\\mathsf T}\\). Multiplication gives \\(XA=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1/2\\\\1&1/2\\end{pmatrix}\\), which produces the same final vector when applied directly to p. Reversing the devices instead gives \\(A(Xp)=(5/8,3/8)^{\\mathsf T}\\), so this pair also demonstrates why temporal order matters.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>One input is not a proof.</strong> Identity and flip both leave the uniform distribution \\((1/2,1/2)^{\\mathsf T}\\) unchanged. Are they the same operation?\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>No. On input \\(|0\\rangle\\), identity outputs \\(|0\\rangle\\) while flip outputs \\(|1\\rangle\\). Agreement on one vector is weaker than agreement as operators. Checking their action on both bit basis vectors would distinguish them.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>Three operations.</strong> A process applies \\(A\\), then \\(B\\), then \\(C\\). Write the combined matrix. If all are column-stochastic, explain why their product is valid.\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>The combined matrix is \\(CBA\\). The closure proof shows \\(BA\\) is column-stochastic, and applying it again shows \\(C(BA)\\) is column-stochastic. Associativity allows the grouping; no factor has been interchanged.</p></details></li>\n<li><strong>What history must be modelled?</strong> A second device behaves differently depending on the original input, even when the same intermediate bit reaches it. Can a single matrix conditioned only on the intermediate bit describe both cases?\n<details><summary>Worked solution</summary><p>Not in general. If the same represented intermediate state leads to different conditional output rules depending on omitted history, that bit alone is not a sufficient state description for the proposed composition model. Include the relevant history/environment label or explicitly specify a different operation for each context.</p></details></li></ol></section>\n<section id=\"readiness\"><h2>7. Chapter checkpoint and the road to quantum computing</h2>\n<p>You now have a complete classical calculation pattern: declare the state labels and order; write a probability vector; represent conditional transitions by stochastic columns; compose the matrices in temporal order; interpret the resulting distribution. You should be able to explain each step without relying on a simulation.</p>\n<ul><li>Represent a definite state and an uncertain description in the same declared basis.</li><li>Distinguish a probability, an empirical frequency, and a conditional probability.</li><li>Build an operation from its response to every definite input.</li><li>Derive \\(p'=Mp\\) and explain why normalization is preserved.</li><li>Track intermediate states and show an explicit noncommuting example.</li></ul>\n<p>Keep the four one-bit functions and the rule “write the intermediate state” in mind. They will return when we work through Deutsch’s algorithm. Knowing what the classical functions do will help us ask a different question about an unknown function.</p><p>Next we develop the complex-number and inner-product language needed for quantum states. Matrix composition will still matter, but the allowed states, operations and measurement rules will change. We will introduce the mathematics where it helps us make that change precisely, while keeping the route toward the classical ML bridge and QML visible.</p>\n</section>\n"}], "legacyLessons": [{"id": "states", "title": "States, amplitudes & measurement", "part": "01 · Quantum foundations", "status": "Draft lesson", "time": "25 min", "source": "Lecture Slides - 1 to 7.pdf, PDF pages 14–20; Practice Problems Q1–5, Q10; introductory transcript used for teaching context.", "objectives": ["Normalize a complex state vector.", "Distinguish amplitude from probability.", "Explain why phase can matter even when Z-basis probabilities do not change."], "body": "<h2>From probabilities to amplitudes</h2><p>A classical random bit is described by probabilities that sum to one. A pure qubit is described by two complex amplitudes: <strong>|ψ⟩ = α|0⟩ + β|1⟩</strong>. Their squared magnitudes sum to one: |α|² + |β|² = 1.</p><p>Computational-basis measurement returns 0 with probability |α|² and 1 with probability |β|². One measurement gives one classical outcome; it does not reveal both amplitudes.</p><h2>A worked example</h2><p>For |ψ⟩ = [|0⟩ + (1 + 3i)|1⟩]/√11, the squared magnitudes are 1/11 and (1² + 3²)/11 = 10/11. They add to one, so the state is normalized. This works through practice question Q1.</p><aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>Keep this distinction</strong><p>Amplitudes can interfere. Probabilities are real, nonnegative numbers obtained after taking squared magnitudes.</p></aside><h2>Explore a pure qubit</h2><p>Up to global phase, |ψ⟩ = cos(θ/2)|0⟩ + exp(iφ) sin(θ/2)|1⟩. The Bloch coordinates are (sin θ cos φ, sin θ sin φ, cos θ). Change φ while holding θ fixed. Predict which measurement probabilities will change.</p>", "lab": "bloch", "quiz": {"question": "With θ = π/2, changing φ from 0 to π changes which quantity?", "options": ["The Z-basis probability of 1", "The X-basis probability of +", "The normalization"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "Z-basis probabilities remain 1/2. The state changes from |+⟩ to |−⟩, so the X-basis probability of + changes from 1 to 0."}, "exercise": "Solve Q2–5 and Q10 in your practice sheet. For Q2, use |1−i|² = 2 before normalizing.", "solution": "Q2: N = 1/√6 and vector (1−i, 2)ᵀ/√6. Q10: θ=π/2, φ=π gives |−⟩.", "notebook": "01-qubit-foundations.ipynb"}, {"id": "gates", "title": "Gates, phase & interference", "part": "01 · Quantum foundations", "status": "Draft lesson", "time": "25 min", "source": "Lecture Slides - 1 to 7.pdf, PDF pages 21–28 and 49–54; Practice Problems Q6–9.", "objectives": ["Apply gates in circuit order.", "Convert relative phase into measurement statistics.", "Recognize global-phase equivalence."], "body": "<h2>Operations preserve normalization</h2><p>A closed-system quantum gate is unitary: U†U = I. The Hadamard gate maps |0⟩ to |+⟩ and |1⟩ to |−⟩. The Z gate leaves |0⟩ unchanged and negates the amplitude of |1⟩.</p><h2>Work through H → Z → H</h2><p>Start in |0⟩. The first H prepares (|0⟩+|1⟩)/√2. Z changes this to (|0⟩−|1⟩)/√2. The final H gives |1⟩. The phase difference becomes a deterministic measurement outcome. Matrix multiplication writes the same sequence as HZH|0⟩, with the rightmost operator acting first.</p><aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>Same probabilities, different states</strong><p>|+⟩ and |−⟩ have identical computational-basis measurement probabilities. Applying H before measurement distinguishes them.</p></aside><h2>Use the existing gate lab</h2><p>Try Pauli Z on |+⟩, then compare with Z on |0⟩. Predict the end point before playing each rotation. The supplied animation is retained as a separate lab.</p><p><a href=\"animations/pauli_gate_bloch_sphere_animation.html\">Open Pauli gate / Bloch sphere lab ↗</a></p>", "lab": null, "quiz": {"question": "What is the result of H → Z → H applied to |0⟩?", "options": ["|0⟩", "|1⟩", "A 50/50 Z-basis distribution"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "H prepares |+⟩, Z turns it into |−⟩, and H maps |−⟩ to |1⟩."}, "exercise": "Solve Q6–9. Compare XZ|ψ⟩ and ZX|ψ⟩: do their different signs imply distinguishable physical states?", "solution": "XZ = −ZX. For any input the outputs differ by global phase −1, so they give identical statistics for all measurements.", "notebook": "01-qubit-foundations.ipynb"}, {"id": "entanglement", "title": "Two qubits & entanglement", "part": "01 · Quantum foundations", "status": "Draft lesson", "time": "30 min", "source": "Lecture Slides - 1 to 7.pdf, PDF pages 29–47; Practice Problems Q11–25.", "objectives": ["Expand tensor products in a fixed basis order.", "Test pure two-qubit states for separability.", "Prepare and measure a Bell state in Qiskit."], "body": "<h2>Two systems, one state</h2><p>We use basis order |00⟩, |01⟩, |10⟩, |11⟩. A two-qubit pure state is a|00⟩ + b|01⟩ + c|10⟩ + d|11⟩. Product states factor into two single-qubit vectors. For a nonzero normalized pure state, factorization is possible exactly when ad − bc = 0.</p><h2>Build a Bell state</h2><p>Begin at |00⟩. Apply H to the left qubit, giving (|00⟩+|10⟩)/√2. A CNOT controlled by that qubit gives (|00⟩+|11⟩)/√2. Here ad−bc=1/2, so the state is entangled.</p><p>Each individual bit is random in computational-basis measurement, but their outcomes always agree. Correlations in one measurement basis alone are not proof of entanglement: a classical mixture of 00 and 11 has those correlations too.</p><aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>Watch the wire order</strong><p>Qiskit displays q₀ at the top, but a two-qubit ket is written |q₁q₀⟩. To implement the left-qubit Bell construction above, use h(1), then cx(1, 0).</p></aside><h2>Partial measurement</h2><p>Project onto the observed outcome and divide surviving amplitudes by the square root of its probability. For a Bell state, observing left bit 0 leaves |00⟩ and observing 1 leaves |11⟩. A zero-probability outcome has no conditional state.</p><p><a href=\"animations/quantum_computing_interactive_companion.html\">Open tensor, measurement & Bell-state companion ↗</a></p>", "lab": null, "quiz": {"question": "For a normalized pure state with coefficients a,b,c,d, when is it separable?", "options": ["a+b+c+d = 1", "ad−bc = 0", "All four probabilities are equal"], "answer": 1, "explanation": "The 2×2 coefficient matrix has rank one exactly when its determinant ad−bc vanishes. This criterion applies to pure two-qubit states."}, "exercise": "Solve Q15, Q16, Q20 and Q25. Then compare Bell-state measurements in Z and X bases in the notebook.", "solution": "Q16: P(left=0)=1/2 and P(left=1)=1/2. Conditional full states are (|00⟩+|01⟩)/√2 and |11⟩. Q25: q₀=1, q₁=0 means |01⟩.", "notebook": "02-bell-states.ipynb"}, {"id": "classifier", "title": "A first variational classifier", "part": "04 · Learning with circuits", "status": "Draft lesson", "time": "35 min", "source": "Lecture 10 Building a Quantum Classifier 1.pdf, PDF pages 17–22, 31 and 33–42. Supplemental one-qubit example.", "objectives": ["Separate data encoding from trainable parameters.", "Read a probability as a model prediction.", "Compute an expectation gradient by parameter shift."], "body": "<h2>A quantum model inside a classical loop</h2><p>Encode a scalar feature x with Rᵧ(x), apply a trainable Rᵧ(w), then measure. Starting from |0⟩, the probability of label 1 is p(1|x,w) = sin²((x+w)/2). A classical optimizer updates w to reduce a loss over training examples.</p><p>This tiny model combines the two rotations into one. It is an intentionally limited teaching model that a classical computer simulates easily. It demonstrates the training loop and makes no claim of quantum advantage.</p><h2>From probability to expectation</h2><p>The Z expectation is f(x,w)=1−2p(1|x,w)=cos(x+w). For this rotation, ∂f/∂w = [f(x,w+π/2)−f(x,w−π/2)]/2. A loss gradient still needs the chain rule; shifting the whole loss is not generally the same calculation.</p><aside class=\"takeaway\"><strong>Evaluate the complete experiment</strong><p>Fit preprocessing on training data only. Keep a test set for final evaluation. Compare classical baselines and report circuit depth, sampling cost, seeds, and uncertainty.</p></aside><h2>Explore before training</h2><p>Move x and w to predict the circuit output. The notebook implements Qiskit statevector predictions, parameter-shift gradients, a small training loop, and a held-out comparison.</p>", "lab": "classifier", "quiz": {"question": "For x=0 and w=π, what is p(1)?", "options": ["0", "1/2", "1"], "answer": 2, "explanation": "Rᵧ(π)|0⟩ = |1⟩, so sin²(π/2)=1."}, "exercise": "Derive ∂p(1|x,w)/∂w. Explain why changing the number of shots changes uncertainty but not the ideal probability.", "solution": "∂p/∂w = sin(x+w)/2. For N independent shots the estimated probability has variance p(1−p)/N.", "notebook": "03-variational-classifier.ipynb"}], "planned": [{"title": "Chapter 2 · Mathematical language", "needs": "Complex numbers, inner products and Dirac notation, developed near their first use."}, {"title": "Chapter 3 · Qubits and measurement", "needs": "Pure states, Born probabilities, phase, and conditional projection."}, {"title": "Chapter 4 · Gates, interference and measurement bases", "needs": "Unitary evolution, gates, rotations, and Bloch geometry."}, {"title": "Chapter 5 · Composite systems and entanglement", "needs": "Tensor products, controlled gates, Bell states, and partial measurement."}, {"title": "Chapter 6 · Registers, conventions and oracles", "needs": "Circuit/readout ordering and reversible oracle access."}, {"title": "Chapter 7 · Learning from quantum algorithms", "needs": "Fully worked kickback and Deutsch; a promised Deutsch–Jozsa example. Simon is an optional extension."}, {"title": "Chapter 8 · Observables, statistics and noisy devices", "needs": "Readout and finite-shot estimates before QML use, with linked depth on mixtures, reduced states and noise."}, {"title": "Part II · Classical machine learning bridge", "needs": "Learning loops, optimization, feature spaces, kernels, and generalization."}, {"title": "Part III · Quantum machine learning", "needs": "Encoding, variational classifiers, quantum kernels, and responsible evaluation."}]};
